    },
    {
      "claim": "The artillery attacks were disproportionate because the expected civilian harm was clearly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated, violating the proportionality rule.",
      "reasoning": "The Chamber’s evidence showed no identifiable military targets and documented massive civilian displacement, indicating that the anticipated military advantage was minimal while the incidental civilian loss was substantial. Launching an attack with knowledge that such loss would be excessive breaches the proportionality prohibition in the Rome Statute.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(iv)",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally launching an attack in the knowledge that such attack will cause incidental loss of life or injury to civilians or damage to civilian objects or widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment which would be clearly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct overall military advantage anticipated;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The artillery attacks caused extensive destruction of property not justified by military necessity, constituting a war crime under the Rome Statute.",
      "reasoning": "The indiscriminate shelling resulted in widespread damage to civilian infrastructure and property in the four towns. The Trial Chamber found no legitimate military purpose for such destruction, satisfying the element of ‘extensive destruction… not justified by military necessity’ as defined in the Statute.",
